## 4. Understanding the Mathematics

### Voltage Divider Rule

The voltage divider rule is used to calculate the voltage across each resistor in a series circuit.
     
4.1 Voltage Divider Rule (VDR)

The voltage divider rule is used to calculate the voltage across individual resistors connected in series.

General formulas:

Voltage across R1:

V_R1 = V_in × [R1 / (R1 + R2)]

Voltage across R2:

V_R2 = V_in × [R2 / (R1 + R2)]

Where:

V_in = Input supply voltage

R1, R2 = Series resistances

V_R1, V_R2 = Voltage drops across the resistors

4.2 Given Values

V_in = 10 V

R1 = 10 kΩ

R2 = 20 kΩ

4.3 Calculation of Total Resistance

For series-connected resistors:

R_T = R1 + R2

R_T = 10 kΩ + 20 kΩ

R_T = 30 kΩ

4.4 Calculation of Circuit Current

Using Ohm's Law:

I = V_in / R_T

I = 10 / 30,000

I = 0.000333 A

I = 0.333 mA

4.5 Calculation of Voltage Across R1

V_R1 = V_in × [R1 / (R1 + R2)]

V_R1 = 10 × [10 / (10 + 20)]

V_R1 = 10 × (10 / 30)

V_R1 = 3.33 V

4.6 Calculation of Output Voltage Across R2

V_out = V_R2 = V_in × [R2 / (R1 + R2)]

V_out = 10 × [20 / (10 + 20)]

V_out = 10 × (20 / 30)

V_out = 6.67 V

4.7 Verification Using Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL)

According to KVL, the sum of voltage drops in a closed loop equals the supply voltage.

V_in = V_R1 + V_R2

10 = 3.33 + 6.67

10 V = 10 V
|Parameter |	Theoretical Result|
|Input Voltage |(Vin)	10 V|
|R1	| 10 kΩ|
|R2	| 20 kΩ|
|Total Resistance (RT)	| 30 kΩ|
|Circuit Current (I)	 | 0.333 mA|
|Voltage across R1 (VR1)	|  3.33 V|
|Output Voltage (Vout)	 | 6.67 V|

This verifies that the sum of the voltage drops equals the input supply voltage.

